WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:05.209
Underneath our rendering section of the project settings,

2
00:00:05.209 --> 00:00:07.650
there are two options I want to call out

3
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:12.929
as they have a direct impact on how optimization works,

4
00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:16.329
but also how your project is going to look

5
00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:20.010
and how your content needs to be adjusted

6
00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:24.210
once you've imported it inside of Unreal Engine 5.

7
00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.449
First thing I want to talk about is virtual textures.

8
00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:30.809
Now, virtual textures come in two different flavors,

9
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:36.250
and they allow you to have basically massive textures

10
00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:40.170
with a ton of detail depending on what you are doing.

11
00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.439
Now, you do need to enable virtual texture support

12
00:00:44.439 --> 00:00:46.840
by clicking on the checkmark button.

13
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.719
Once you do that, you will need to restart the engine,

14
00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:51.280
and it will be enabled.

15
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.240
Now, again, the two flavors or different types

16
00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.719
of virtual textures are runtime virtual texture support

17
00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:02.479
and streaming virtual texture support.

18
00:01:02.479 --> 00:01:03.640
The main difference between the two

19
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.760
is that runtime virtual texture support

20
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.640
supports things that are procedurally generated

21
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.439
that need to be, say, baked down

22
00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:12.760
in order to be more performant.

23
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:14.879
This is going to be things like landscapes

24
00:01:14.879 --> 00:01:18.579
and any procedurally generated content.

25
00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:21.500
Streaming virtual textures allow you

26
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.790
to stream in massive textures, often accompanied

27
00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:27.950
with a UDIM workflow.

28
00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:31.909
Now, one last note about the difference between these two

29
00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:35.829
is that our runtime virtually texturing

30
00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:39.230
is going to give us performance back

31
00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:40.950
at the cost of memory.

32
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.829
And our streaming virtual texturing method

33
00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:45.230
is going to give us memory back

34
00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.310
at the cost of performance.

35
00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:50.230
So again, if we're doing something, say, real time

36
00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:51.829
where we have a player, we're probably

37
00:01:51.829 --> 00:01:54.590
going to want to use the runtime virtual texturing.

38
00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.430
And if we're doing something cinematic or something

39
00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:00.189
like that, we could use the streaming virtual texturing

40
00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:01.409
setup.

41
00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:03.650
The next thing in rendering I want to talk about

42
00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.650
and going to be the last thing is shadows.

43
00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.330
By default, virtual shadow maps are enabled.

44
00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.810
And this is the recommended shadowing method

45
00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.849
you should be using when it comes to Unreal Engine 5.

46
00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:20.930
Virtual shadow maps give you a much higher resolution shadow.

47
00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.729
And they also are meant to work

48
00:02:23.729 --> 00:02:28.289
with things like world partition, neonite, and lumen.
